#!/usr/bin/env bash
[[ ! ${WARDEN_DIR} ]] && >&2 echo -e "\033[31mThis script is not intended to be run directly!\033[0m" && exit 1

WARDEN_USAGE=$(cat <<EOF
\033[33mUsage:\033[0m
  warden worktree [path]

\033[33mDescription:\033[0m
  Gives an existing git worktree a Warden environment of its own, so several
  branches of one project can run at the same time without colliding on
  environment name, domain or container names.

  Creating the worktree is herdr's job — see 'herdr worktree create', which
  places checkouts under \033[32m\$WORKTREES/<repo>/<branch>\033[0m. This
  command configures one that already exists. It normally runs on its own,
  from the herdr plugin bound to the worktree.created event; run it by hand
  to configure a worktree made some other way, or to redo one.

  With no argument it works on the current directory. The parent checkout is
  found through git, and its config read for the project name and domain. The
  environment becomes \033[32m<project>-<directory>\033[0m and the domain
  \033[32m<project>-<directory>.<base domain>\033[0m.

  The name and domain are written to the worktree's \033[32m.local\033[0m
  override, which Warden loads after the main config and lets override
  WARDEN_, TRAEFIK_ and PHP_ values. Application-level settings such as
  APP_URL are not touched.

  A parent that is not a Warden project is reported and skipped, not treated
  as an error: the command runs against every worktree herdr creates.

  The path is the only thing printed on stdout, so it can be captured.

\033[33mConfig filenames:\033[0m
  Warden reads \033[32m.warden.env\033[0m, falling back to the deprecated
  \033[32m.env\033[0m, and pairs the \033[32m.local\033[0m override with
  whichever it resolved. This command follows the parent's choice rather than
  imposing one, so a project on .warden.env gets .warden.env.local and one
  still on .env gets .env.local.

  A committed .warden.env arrives with the checkout, so there is nothing to
  copy. A project still on .env has it gitignored — Warden cannot detect an
  environment without one — so the parent's is copied in. An existing config
  in the worktree is left alone either way.

\033[33mConfiguration:\033[0m
  WARDEN_WORKTREE_DOMAIN  Base domain for worktree environments. The worktree
                          is given \033[32m<project>-<directory>\033[0m as a
                          subdomain of it.

                          Read from the parent's config first, then from
                          Warden's own \033[32m~/.warden/.env\033[0m, so one
                          line there — WARDEN_WORKTREE_DOMAIN=stowbox.dev —
                          covers every project on the machine and a project
                          that needs a different domain overrides it locally.

                          Set in neither, it falls back to the parent's
                          TRAEFIK_DOMAIN with its first label removed, so
                          kmstools-checkout-lunar.stowbox.dev yields
                          stowbox.dev.

\033[33mSee also:\033[0m
  The directory herdr creates worktrees in is worktrees.directory in
  ~/.config/herdr/config.toml. Keep it in step with \$WORKTREES.
EOF
)
